Title: Aligning the tabular representation of ASN.1 with PRD13 section 4.6 Reason for change: RAN5 has agreed rules for tabular representation in RAN5 specs of the RRC messages specified in ASN.1 in RAN5 specs (e.g. TS 38.331). These rules are defined in RAN5 PRD13 and have been applied since the introduction of the LTE Conformance testing. However, some of these rules have not been applied consistently when 5G RRC messages have been specified in TS 38.508-1. This from one side lowers the quality of the RAN5 specs but from another provides room for confusion and misinterpretation of the RRC messages definitions. RAN5 raised an AP#86.03 calling for a solution. Examples of rules which have not been followed: (1) The conversion into a tabular format of the ASN.1 definitions for SEQUENCE OF TYPE where the TYPE is a locally or externally defined User defined ASN.1 type for example drb-CountMSB-InfoList SEQUENCE (SIZE (1..maxDRB)) OF DRB-CountMSB-Info, where DRB-CountMSB-Info itself is defined as DRB-CountMSB-Info SEQUENCE (2) The ASN.1 symbol "::=" shall be used only at the very top level of the element definition (on the parent name) and not in the definition of the sub-fields (3) Indentation rules for sub-elements (4) Groups of elements shall be identified by enclosing them between brackets { ... } (5) Invisible rows borders for elements with multiple alternative values (6) No need for type definition for elements which are set to 'Not Present' Other issues found: redundant spaces, paragraphs, spec TS 38.508-1 referred to from TS 38.508-1, non 3GPP styles. Summary of change: Corrections have been applied throughout the spec to align the messages definitions with the RAN5 agreed rules for tabular representation in RAN5 specs of the RRC messages specified in ASN.1. Editorial error repaired. Consequences if not approved: Low quality of RAN5 specs. Inconsistency. Differences between the representation on the prose and the implementation in TTCN. Possibility for misinterpretation of the required content of RRC messages which can lead to compliant devices being unfairly failed during testing, or/and, non-compliant devices passing the tests. Clauses affected: 4.6
